</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem
<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>Partially supported by the
DFG-Schwerpunkt “Komplexe Mannigfaltigkeiten” and by the DFG Leibniz Preis.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hélène Esnault
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>
Universität Duisburg-Essen, FB6, Mathematik, 45117 Essen, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:esnault@uni-essen.de">esnault@uni-essen.de</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_dedicatory">À Michael Artin, à l’occasion de son 70-ième anniversaire
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: June 20, 2004</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We give a proof of Artin’s vanishing theorem in characteristic zero, based on
Deligne’s Riemann-Hilbert correspondence.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="p1.1" class="ltx_quote">
</blockquote>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Corollaire 3.5, M. Artin shows that if <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a affine
variety over a separably closed field <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and if
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constructible sheaf on it, then étale cohomology
<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}_{\acute{e}t}(X,{\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>´</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}_{\acute{e}t}(X,{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> is vanishing for <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq{\rm dim}(X)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq{\rm dim}(X)+1</annotation></semantics></math>. He reduces the proof to dimension 1 by applying base change for proper morphisms. The purpose of this note is to give an analytic proof of Artin’s
famous vanishing theorem in the analytic category, that is when <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the field of complex numbers, <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constructible sheaf of complex vector spaces
with possibly infinite monodromies on strata, and the cohomology is the analytic one. Surely this is not necessary to have an analytic proof to apply Artin’s vanishing theorem analytically, but this is perhaps
of interest to know it exists.
Indeed, we give two proofs in the framework of Deligne’s Riemann-Hilbert correspondence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. The first one is based on a splitting property proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Proposition 1.2. The second one is a slightly different
form of this
and is due to Pierre Deligne. He did not write it down in his Lectures Notes
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>,
but communicated to us in a discussion on the first proof. It is based on
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Proposition 5, p. 412. In both cases, it allows to compute the analytic cohomology of the extension by <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of a local system on a Zariski open set as the hypercohomology of a suitable algebraic
de Rham complex.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Acknowledgement:</span> We thank Pierre Deligne for explaining to us his proof.
The analytic proof of the vanishing theorem was
explained in a course held in the spring 2003 at the University of Essen. We
thank the students for their active participation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry</h2>

<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
be an affine variety defined over the field of complex numbers, and
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a constructible sheaf. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;{\rm dim}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;{\rm dim}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is the support of <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=H^{m}(Y_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=H^{m}(Y_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is affine. Thus we may assume that the support of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Noether normalization, with <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n={\rm dim}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n={\rm dim}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},\pi_{*}{\mathcal{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>an</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},{\mathcal{F}})=H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},\pi_{*}{\mathcal{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constructible sheaf with support <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{A}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{A}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we may assume <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:\emptyset\neq U\hookrightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:\emptyset\neq U\hookrightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math> be a Zariski open set so that <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}|_{U_{{\rm an}}}={\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>an</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}|_{U_{{\rm an}}}={\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a local
system. Thus <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}\subset{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}\subset{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the quotient <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}/j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}/j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a constructible sheaf with support in
dimension <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus inducting on <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dim}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dim}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume
<math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}=j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}=j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as for <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dim}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dim}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math> the theorem is trivial.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=X\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=X\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the constant sheaf <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank of <math id="S2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From the exact sequence
<math id="S2.p1.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\to j_{!}{\mathbb{C}}^{r}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{r}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{r}|_{Y}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to j_{!}{\mathbb{C}}^{r}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{r}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{r}|_{Y}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the induction, we reduce
the theorem to the vanishing of <math id="S2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},{\mathbb{C}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>an</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},{\mathbb{C}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is trivial.
Else <math id="S2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor. Then there is an algebraic bundle <math id="S2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (which has to be trivial since <math id="S2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) with an algebraic connection <math id="S2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla:V\to\Omega^{1}_{X}(mY)\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">∇</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla:V\to\Omega^{1}_{X}(mY)\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math> with meromorphic poles along <math id="S2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, such that
<math id="S2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{{\rm an}}|_{U})^{\nabla}={\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∇</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{{\rm an}}|_{U})^{\nabla}={\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We choose for <math id="S2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math> any extension to <math id="S2.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of the
unique regular singular connection on <math id="S2.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\setminus Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\setminus Y</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Théorème 5.9). In particular, it is regular singular “at the <math id="S2.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>”.
Let <math id="S2.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the ideal sheaf of <math id="S2.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in{\mathbb{N}},N&gt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in{\mathbb{N}},N&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, one defines the complex</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{N}:{\mathcal{I}}^{N}\otimes V\to{\mathcal{I}}^{N-1}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{1}_{X}(mY)\to\ldots\\
\ldots\to{\mathcal{I}}^{N-n}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{n}_{X}(nmY)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{N}:{\mathcal{I}}^{N}\otimes V\to{\mathcal{I}}^{N-1}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{1}_{X}(mY)\to\ldots\\
\ldots\to{\mathcal{I}}^{N-n}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{n}_{X}(nmY)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> large, <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct summand of
<math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">(See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Proposition 1.2).
Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathbb{P}}\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathbb{P}}\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a birational projective morphism, with <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> smooth,
so that <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Z}+H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Z}+H</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing divisor. Here we denote by
<math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> the inverse image <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> its Zariski closure
in <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> the inverse image <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also set <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:{\mathbb{A}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n},\tilde{\lambda}:\pi^{-1}({\mathbb{A}}^{n})={\mathbb{P}}\setminus H\to{\mathbb{P}},j:U\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n},\tilde{j}:U\to{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:{\mathbb{A}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n},\tilde{\lambda}:\pi^{-1}({\mathbb{A}}^{n})={\mathbb{P}}\setminus H\to{\mathbb{P}},j:U\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n},\tilde{j}:U\to{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{V},\bar{\nabla})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∇</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{V},\bar{\nabla})</annotation></semantics></math> be an extension to <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free and
<math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\nabla}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∇</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\nabla}</annotation></semantics></math> has logarithmic poles along <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Z}+H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Z}+H</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> large enough, <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R\tilde{\lambda}_{*}\tilde{j}_{!}V\to(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}}(\log(\bar{Z}+H))(-M\bar{Z})\otimes\bar{V})_{{\rm an}}\to(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}}(\log\bar{Z})(*H)(-M\bar{Z})\otimes\bar{V})_{{\rm an}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>an</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>an</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\tilde{\lambda}_{*}\tilde{j}_{!}V\to(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}}(\log(\bar{Z}+H))(-M\bar{Z})\otimes\bar{V})_{{\rm an}}\to(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}}(\log\bar{Z})(*H)(-M\bar{Z})\otimes\bar{V})_{{\rm an}}</annotation></semantics></math> are quasi-isomorphisms
(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Corollaire 6.10, and via duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, (2.9), (2.11)), thus by GAGA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, it induces an isomorphism
<math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}({\mathbb{P}}\setminus H,\tilde{j}_{!}{\mathcal{V}})(=H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}))\xrightarrow{\cong}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}\setminus H,\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H}(\log Z)(-MZ)\otimes\bar{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>j</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo></mover><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}({\mathbb{P}}\setminus H,\tilde{j}_{!}{\mathcal{V}})(=H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}}))\xrightarrow{\cong}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}\setminus H,\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H}(\log Z)(-MZ)\otimes\bar{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking now any coherent extension <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> being the ideal sheaf of the Zariski closure <math id="S2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one has <math id="S2.p2.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{*}{\mathcal{K}}_{N}=({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}{\mathcal{K}}_{N}=({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n}))</annotation></semantics></math>. By GAGA again, one has
<math id="S2.p2.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n},{\mathcal{K}}_{N})={\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})))={\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},(({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})))_{{\rm an}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>an</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n},{\mathcal{K}}_{N})={\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})))={\mathbb{H}}^{m}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},(({\mathcal{I}}^{\prime})^{N-\bullet}\otimes V^{\prime}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(\bullet mY^{\prime})(*({\mathbb{P}}^{n}\setminus{\mathbb{A}}^{n})))_{{\rm an}}).</annotation></semantics></math>
The latter group receives <math id="S2.p2.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},j_{!}V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>an</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}({\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{{\rm an}},j_{!}V)</annotation></semantics></math> while functoriality implies the existence of</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}:{\mathcal{K}}_{N}\to R\pi_{*}(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H}(\log Z)(-MZ)\otimes\bar{V})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}:{\mathcal{K}}_{N}\to R\pi_{*}(\Omega^{\bullet}_{{\mathbb{P}}\setminus H}(\log Z)(-MZ)\otimes\bar{V}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is an isomorphism on <math id="S2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is affine, one has
<math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N})=H^{m}(\Gamma(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N})=H^{m}(\Gamma(X,{\mathcal{K}}_{N}))=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;{\rm dim}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;{\rm dim}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using
lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> this concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rmk">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Of course in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we could replace <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathbb{A}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by any smooth
variety <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now reproduce a communication by P. Deligne, which completes Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Instead of considering (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) for a fixed <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to a splitting of <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j_{!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{!}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the inverse system of such. More generally, let <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a local system on a smooth Zariski open <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset\neq U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset\neq U</annotation></semantics></math>
of a complex variety <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a bounded complex of coherent sheaves, extending
<math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>U</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},\nabla_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},\nabla_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique algebraic bundle with a regular singular connection on <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with underlying <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, loc. cit.
Let <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sheaf of ideals with supports <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=X\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=X\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{L}}_{N})_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{L}}_{N})_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projective system of complexes <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle({\mathcal{L}}_{N})^{p}={\mathcal{I}}^{N-p}{\mathcal{K}}^{p}:={\rm Im}({\mathcal{I}}^{N-p}\otimes_{{\mathcal{O}}_{X}}{\mathcal{K}}^{p}\to{\mathcal{K}}^{p})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle({\mathcal{L}}_{N})^{p}={\mathcal{I}}^{N-p}{\mathcal{K}}^{p}:={\rm Im}({\mathcal{I}}^{N-p}\otimes_{{\mathcal{O}}_{X}}{\mathcal{K}}^{p}\to{\mathcal{K}}^{p}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For example, for <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}=\Omega_{X}^{\bullet}(\bullet mY)\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}=\Omega_{X}^{\bullet}(\bullet mY)\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math> as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, then <math id="S2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is nearly equal to <math id="S2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) (nearly as we haven’t assumed <math id="S2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to be locally free, thus the tensor product is not necessarily equal to the product in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Artin’s vanishing in complex geometry ‣ Variation on Artin’s vanishing theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).) We denote by <math id="S2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:U\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:U\to X</annotation></semantics></math> the open embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(P. Deligne)<span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One has</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})=\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{L}}_{N})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>←</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})=\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{L}}_{N}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">(P. Deligne) Let
<math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\tilde{X}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\tilde{X}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective birational morphism with <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma|_{U}={\rm id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma|_{U}={\rm id}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-1}(Y)=:Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-1}(Y)=:Z</annotation></semantics></math> a normal crossing divisor. Let us set <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}=\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}=\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and choose <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math>
an extension to <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},\nabla_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},\nabla_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Proposition 5,
the projective system <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially constant of value <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that for <math id="S2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> given, there is a <math id="S2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}&gt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}&gt;N</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N^{\prime}}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))\xrightarrow{0}R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">0</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N^{\prime}}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))\xrightarrow{0}R^{a}\sigma_{*}({\mathcal{J}}^{N}\otimes V\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, for all <math id="S2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, there are <math id="S2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i},i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i},i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathcal{L}}_{N_{2}})^{p}\to\sigma_{*}(V\otimes{\mathcal{J}}^{N_{1}}\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))\to({\mathcal{L}}_{N})^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathcal{L}}_{N_{2}})^{p}\to\sigma_{*}(V\otimes{\mathcal{J}}^{N_{1}}\otimes\Omega^{p}_{\tilde{X}}(\log Z))\to({\mathcal{L}}_{N})^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This shows that in the proposition, we may assume that <math id="S2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
is smooth, <math id="S2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=X\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=X\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal crossing divisor, <math id="S2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}^{\bullet}=V\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}^{\bullet}=V\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math> extends <math id="S2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},\nabla_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},\nabla_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> locally free and <math id="S2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> with logarithmic poles.
Again for all <math id="S2.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, there there are <math id="S2.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i},i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i},i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S2.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{N_{2}}\to V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-N_{1}Z)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))\to{\mathcal{L}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{N_{2}}\to V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-N_{1}Z)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))\to{\mathcal{L}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{L}}_{N})=\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>←</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>lim</mi><mo>←</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,{\mathcal{L}}_{N})=\varprojlim_{N}{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Taking <math id="S2.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}\supset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⊃</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}\supset X</annotation></semantics></math> a good compactification with
<math id="S2.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty=\bar{X}\setminus X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty=\bar{X}\setminus X</annotation></semantics></math> a normal crossing divisor, and
with <math id="S2.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{V},\bar{\nabla})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∇</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{V},\bar{\nabla})</annotation></semantics></math> an extension to <math id="S2.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\nabla)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\nabla)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p5.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{V}</annotation></semantics></math> locally free and <math id="S2.p5.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\nabla}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∇</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\nabla}</annotation></semantics></math> with logarithmic poles,
one has
<math id="S2.p5.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))={\mathbb{H}}^{m}(\bar{X},\bar{V}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{\tilde{X}}(-N\bar{Z})\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{\bar{X}}(\log\bar{Z})(*\infty))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X,V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))={\mathbb{H}}^{m}(\bar{X},\bar{V}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{\tilde{X}}(-N\bar{Z})\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{\bar{X}}(\log\bar{Z})(*\infty))</annotation></semantics></math>. Here
<math id="S2.p5.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Z}\subset\bar{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Z}\subset\bar{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Zariski closure of <math id="S2.p5.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Again by GAGA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(\bar{X},\bar{V}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{\bar{X}}(-N\bar{Z})\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{\tilde{X}}(\log\bar{Z})(*\infty))=\\
{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},(V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))_{{\rm an}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>N</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>an</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(\bar{X},\bar{V}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{\bar{X}}(-N\bar{Z})\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{\tilde{X}}(\log\bar{Z})(*\infty))=\\
{\mathbb{H}}^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},(V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{X}(-NZ)\otimes\Omega^{\bullet}_{X}(\log Z))_{{\rm an}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the latter for <math id="S2.p5.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> large enough is equal to <math id="S2.p5.m41" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>an</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{m}(X_{{\rm an}},j_{!}{\mathcal{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Corollaire 6.10, and via duality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, (2.9), (2.11).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
